---
// How TableTalk works, in plain English. The backtest numbers come from
// src/data/backtests.ts (copied from the project README, checked against it
// by a unit test) until contract request R10 publishes them as a data file.
import { BACKTESTS, PREMIER_LEAGUE_LOG_LOSS, README_COMMIT, REPORT_SEASONS } from "../data/backtests.ts";
import { LEAGUES } from "../data/leagues.ts";
import { formatDifference } from "../format/track-record.ts";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/umarjaved204/TableTalk";
const README = `${REPO}/blob/${README_COMMIT}/README.md`;
const whole = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-GB");
const name = (id: string) => LEAGUES.find((l) => l.id === id)?.name ?? id;
const oneDecimal = (x: number) => x.toFixed(1);

const SECTIONS = [
  ["model", "The match model"],
  ["season", "From matches to a season"],
  ["uncertainty", "Why season chances are less extreme"],
  ["promoted", "Promoted teams"],
  ["reading", "How to read the numbers"],
  ["backtests", "Backtests: how well it has done on past seasons"],
  ["limitations", "Known limitations"],
  ["honesty", "Locking and honesty rules"],
  ["source", "Source code and reference"],
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description="How TableTalk works: a Dixon-Coles match model, 10,000 simulated seasons, rating uncertainty, backtests per league, and known limitations."
>
  <div class="container page">
    <header class="head">
      <h1>How TableTalk works</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A match model turns each team's past results into a chance of every scoreline. A simulator plays the
        rest of the season 10,000 times with those chances. Both were tested on past seasons before any live
        prediction was made.
      </p>
      <nav aria-labelledby="toc-heading" class="toc">
        <h2 id="toc-heading" class="toc-heading">
          On this page
        </h2>
        <ol>
          {SECTIONS.map(([id, title]) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section class="part" id="model" aria-labelledby="model-heading">
      <h2 id="model-heading">1. The match model</h2>
      <p>
        TableTalk uses the <strong>Dixon-Coles</strong> model (1997), a small model of goals that can be
        explained in a few sentences:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Every team has two numbers: an <strong>attack</strong> strength and a <strong>defence</strong>{" "}
          strength. One league-wide <strong>home advantage</strong> is added on top.
        </li>
        <li>
          Those numbers give each side's expected goals in a match. They work as multipliers: an attack of
          +0.24 means about 27% more goals than an average team.
        </li>
        <li>
          Goals are then drawn around those expectations (a Poisson distribution), with one small correction:
          real football has more 0–0s and 1–1s, and fewer 1–0s and 0–1s, than that predicts. One extra number,
          called rho, fixes exactly those four scores.
        </li>
        <li>
          The numbers are fitted to past results, with recent matches counting more: a match's weight halves
          every 365 days.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        <strong>Why this model:</strong> a season simulator needs whole scorelines, because goal difference
        decides places, and Dixon-Coles gives a full distribution over scores. Every number in it means
        something you can say out loud. The trade-off: it knows only results, nothing about injuries,
        transfers or managers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="part" id="season" aria-labelledby="season-heading">
      <h2 id="season-heading">2. From matches to a season</h2>
      <p>One simulated season works like this:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Start from the real table.</li>
        <li>Draw this season's team strengths from how uncertain they are (next section).</li>
        <li>Play every remaining fixture by drawing a scoreline from the match model.</li>
        <li>
          Add the simulated results to the real ones and rank the table with the league's own points and
          tie-breaking rules.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That is repeated 10,000 times. A team's title chance is the share of those seasons it finished first;
        “Proj. pts” is its average final points across them. The fixtures come from the real published fixture
        list, never a generated one. Each league's rules (number of teams, tiebreakers, play-off places) are
        settings, and every official final table from 2010-11 to 2025-26 is reproduced exactly from the
        results.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="part" id="uncertainty" aria-labelledby="uncertainty-heading">
      <h2 id="uncertainty-heading">3. Why season chances are less extreme</h2>
      <p>
        We don't know exactly how good each team is. A team that is really a little better than its rating is
        better in <em>all</em> its remaining matches, not just one. So each simulated season draws its own
        strengths, spread according to how well the results pin each team down: wide for promoted teams and
        early in a season, narrow for established sides.
      </p>
      <p>
        Without this, early-season forecasts were over-confident in the Premier League backtest: when it said
        70–99% before a season started, the outcome happened 71% of the time against an average forecast of
        87%. With it, 83% against 86%. That is why a team's season chances are a little less extreme than
        repeating each match's chances would suggest.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="part" id="promoted" aria-labelledby="promoted-heading">
      <h2 id="promoted-heading">4. Promoted teams</h2>
      <p>
        A newly promoted team has few or no results in its new league. Rated on a handful of games, it can
        look far better or worse than it is. So each promoted team starts from the average first-season rating
        of the teams promoted in the previous 15 seasons, and its own results pull it away from there.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two alternatives were tested: no special handling, and rating the second division alongside. In the
        Premier League this approach was consistently better but modest against both (ahead in 10 of 12
        seasons against each, with margins inside the noise in one of the two halves of the data). In the
        other leagues the three were within noise of each other, so the same approach is used everywhere.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="part" id="reading" aria-labelledby="reading-heading">
      <h2 id="reading-heading">5. How to read the numbers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Whole percentages.</strong> With 10,000 simulated seasons, a 50% chance carries about ±1
          point of simulation noise, so a decimal would claim precision that isn't there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Never 0% or 100%.</strong> “Didn't happen in 10,000 simulations” is not the same as
          impossible, so very small chances show as “&lt;1%” and very large ones as “&gt;99%”.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Home, draw and away add up to 100.</strong> They are rounded together so the three shown
          always sum to 100.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Zones are league positions only:</strong> title, top four, top five, top half, relegation
          and, where a league has one, the relegation play-off place. They are not claims about European
          qualification, which also depends on cups that aren't modelled.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Times are in your time zone.</strong> Kick-off times can still change, for example for
          television.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="part" id="backtests" aria-labelledby="backtests-heading">
      <h2 id="backtests-heading">6. Backtests: how well it has done on past seasons</h2>
      <p>
        A backtest replays past seasons week by week: each forecast uses only results from before that week.
        The settings were chosen on 2012-13 to 2017-18, and the results below are from the{" "}
        <strong>report seasons</strong> ({REPORT_SEASONS}), which were never used to choose anything. The two
        seasons affected by COVID are left out, a decision made before any results were seen.
      </p>
      <p>
        Scores are <strong>log loss</strong> (lower is better). Each forecast is compared, on the same
        matches, with base rates (the league's usual home, draw and away frequencies) and with the betting
        market (closing odds with the bookmaker's margin removed, the strongest public forecast). In the
        Premier League the log losses were: model {PREMIER_LEAGUE_LOG_LOSS.model}, base rates{" "}
        {PREMIER_LEAGUE_LOG_LOSS.baseRates}, market {PREMIER_LEAGUE_LOG_LOSS.market}.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="backtest-caption" tabindex="0">
        <table data-backtests>
          <caption id="backtest-caption">Match forecasts, report seasons</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">League</th>
              <th scope="col">Matches</th>
              <th scope="col">Log loss compared with base rates</th>
              <th scope="col">Share of the gap to the market closed</th>
              <th scope="col">Model minus market, log loss (± 95% interval)</th>
              <th scope="col">Calibration gap, home / draw / away (points)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {BACKTESTS.map((b) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{name(b.leagueId)}</th>
                <td>{whole.format(b.matches)}</td>
                <td>{oneDecimal(-b.vsBaseRatesPercent)}% lower*</td>
                <td>{b.gapClosedPercent}%</td>
                <td>{formatDifference(b.minusMarket.diff, b.minusMarket.ci95)}</td>
                <td>
                  {oneDecimal(b.calibrationGap.home)} / {oneDecimal(b.calibrationGap.draw)} /{" "}
                  {oneDecimal(b.calibrationGap.away)}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul class="notes">
        <li>
          <strong>Against the market:</strong> in every league the market's log loss is lower, by about 0.02,
          and every interval excludes zero. The model closes 77–85% of the gap between knowing nothing and the
          market. Part of the gap is information the model never sees: team news, line-ups, managerial
          changes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Calibration gap:</strong> how far, on average, the forecasts sit from “60% happens 60% of
          the time”, in percentage points. Small is good.
        </li>
        <li>
          * <strong>Against base rates</strong> the README gives no interval, so none is shown here. Contract
          request R10 asks for these numbers, with intervals, in a published file.
        </li>
        <li>
          Numbers copied from the project <a href={README}>README at commit {README_COMMIT}</a>, where every
          figure, the method and the code that produced it are written up.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The live <a href="/track-record/">track record</a> is separate: it scores only predictions locked
        before kick-off, and is never mixed with these backtests.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="part" id="limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-heading">
      <h2 id="limitations-heading">7. Known limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing beyond results:</strong> no injuries, suspensions, transfers, managerial changes or
          fixture congestion. A sudden change in a team reaches the ratings only through later results.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ratings lag a team that is genuinely declining.</strong> Late in a season it still partly
          trusts ratings built on last season, and mid-to-late season forecasts are slightly under-confident.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It can't pick out likely draws.</strong> Draw forecasts are honest but almost all sit
          between 20% and 30%; the market's barely vary more.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Future points deductions are not anticipated.</strong> Only decisions already announced are
          applied.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One home advantage per league</strong>, although some grounds are harder to visit than
          others.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ratings can't be compared across leagues.</strong> Each league is fitted on its own, so a
          rating is relative to that league's average.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Early-season forecasts lean on last season</strong>, because there are few results yet.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="part" id="honesty" aria-labelledby="honesty-heading">
      <h2 id="honesty-heading">8. Locking and honesty rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Every night the newest prediction for each upcoming match is saved. The last one saved before
          kick-off is the match's <strong>locked prediction</strong>, and only locked predictions are scored.
        </li>
        <li>
          Postponed matches have their lock voided and get a new one before the new kick-off. A prediction
          found to be from after the actual kick-off is not counted. Nothing is back-dated.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every lock records the exact version of the model that made it. Each line of the lock log carries a
          fingerprint of the line before it, so editing an old line shows.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every night's files are published to a public, protected branch: strong evidence of when each
          prediction was made, not proof, since it rests on GitHub's records.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The scored results are on the <a href="/track-record/">track record</a> page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="part" id="source" aria-labelledby="source-heading">
      <h2 id="source-heading">9. Source code and reference</h2>
      <p>
        All the code, the backtests and their write-up are public:{" "}
        <a href={REPO}>github.com/umarjaved204/TableTalk</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="reference">
        Dixon, M. J. and Coles, S. G. (1997).{" "}
        <em>Modelling Association Football Scores and Inefficiencies in the Football Betting Market.</em>{" "}
        Journal of the Royal Statistical Society, Series C (Applied Statistics), 46(2), 265–280.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* A long link (a URL) may break anywhere rather than widen the page. */
  .page a {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .page {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    padding-block: var(--space-6) 0;
  }

  .head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .lede {
    max-width: 70ch;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }

  .toc {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }

  .toc-heading {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
  }

  .toc ol {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0 var(--space-5);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 280px), 1fr));
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.4em;
  }

  .toc a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 32px;
  }

  .part {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    min-width: 0;
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-2);
    background: var(--surface);
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .part h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }

  .part p,
  .part ol,
  .part ul {
    max-width: 75ch;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .part ol,
  .part ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: 1.4em;
  }

  .notes {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  caption {
    padding-block: var(--space-1);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-align: left;
  }

  th,
  td {
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    text-align: right;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  thead th {
    font-weight: 600;
    vertical-align: bottom;
    white-space: normal;
    min-width: 7em;
  }

  tbody th {
    text-align: left;
  }

  .reference {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
